  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Evaluate the double integral ${\u222c}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,2]\\times [2,4].$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\u222c}_{R}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A+{\u222c}_{R}6y\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =(4-2){\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{x}{|}_{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{2}-(-\\frac{2}{1})) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}6y\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(2-1){\\int }_{2}^{4}6y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =3({4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=2+36=38.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
